{"authored" [{"question" "q-73156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is ${\\int }_{e}^{3e}ln⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929463/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f2e6346d752ba1e44954efae4a0af643c4d4fd5ff51f101d8fe611b55022167" "basis" 312 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929463/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{e}^{3e}ln⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=ln⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=e,$ and the upper bound is $b=3e.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3e-e}{n}=\\frac{2e}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{3e}ln⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(e+i\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(e+\\frac{2ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[ln⁡(e+\\frac{2ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2e}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929463/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f2e6346d752ba1e44954efae4a0af643c4d4fd5ff51f101d8fe611b55022167" "basis" 312 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929463/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "m